{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>כְּמִשְׁפַּט הַבָּנוֹת יַעֲשֶׂה לָּהּ</b> — On the level of peshat, it is possible to say that if the purchaser designates her for his son — and this is the betrothal with which he betroths her to him — for יִעוּד (designation) is an expression of arranging an appointment, as in 'from the appointed time that he designated' (II Samuel 20:5). 'According to the judgment that a man does for his daughters he shall do for her' — that he shall give her from his own property like the bride-price of virgins. And He commanded in this just as He commanded regarding the dismissal-gift, and all is kindness from Him, may He be exalted. And according to our Sages — and this is the truth — <b>כְּמִשְׁפַּט הַבָּנוֹת</b> means: as the fathers marry off their daughters, so shall the son do for her. And the explanation of their judgment is that if he takes another, <b>שְׁאֵרָהּ כְּסוּתָהּ וְעוֹנָתָהּ</b> of this one <b>לֹא יִגְרָע</b> (he shall not diminish), and there is no need to say 'if he does not take another,' for Scripture speaks of what is present.\n\nAnd Rashi explained: <b>שְׁאֵרָהּ</b> — food; <b>כְּסוּתָהּ</b> — as its meaning; <b>עוֹנָתָהּ</b> — conjugal relations. And so Onkelos said 'זִיּוּנַהּ' (her ornamentation/relations). And in the Gemara (Ketuvot 47b) they said concerning one who said thus — 'And this Tanna holds that food-allowance is from the Torah, as it is taught: שְׁאֵרָהּ — these are food-allowances,' and so it says, 'And He rained upon them like dust of שְׁאֵר' (Psalms 78:27) etc. And what is understood from the context of the Gemara is that these are the words of an individual, and the halakhah is that the Rabbis enacted food-allowance for her. Also on the level of peshat, why would Scripture mention food-allowances with the word שְׁאֵר, which is flesh, when it would be fitting to mention her bread, for by bread does a man live, and on it would the obligation rest? And Rabbi Eliyahu thought to correct this, and explained שְׁאֵרָהּ as food, so that שְׁאֵרָהּ would mean her flesh. But there is no sense in this, that Scripture would say the husband shall not diminish her flesh.\n\nTherefore I say that the meaning of שְׁאֵר in every place is flesh that cleaves and is close to a man's own flesh, derived from the root שְׁאֵרִית (remainder), that is, the rest of his flesh apart from the flesh of his own body, and relatives are called שְׁאֵר: 'to all שְׁאֵר of his flesh' (Leviticus 18:6), 'her שְׁאֵר are these' (Leviticus 18:17), in the sense of 'but you are my bone and my flesh' (Genesis 29:14), 'and he ate half his flesh' (Numbers 12:12). And likewise 'name and שְׁאֵר, offspring and descendant' (Isaiah 14:22) — his seed that is close to him. And likewise 'when your flesh and your שְׁאֵר are consumed' (Proverbs 5:11) — yourself and your children, who are the flesh close to you. And flesh that is eaten may also be called שְׁאֵר: 'and He rained upon them like dust of שְׁאֵר' (Psalms 78:27), for the flesh eaten cleaves to the eater and turns into his flesh. And it is possible that from this comes 'when your flesh and your שְׁאֵר are consumed' (Proverbs 5:11) — the flesh of your body and the rest of the flesh made from food will be consumed and will not become flesh for you. And the woman is called שְׁאֵר to the husband, as they expounded (Yevamot 22a): 'but only to his שְׁאֵר' (Leviticus 21:2) — his שְׁאֵר is his wife, and this is from the concept that Scripture stated: 'and he shall cleave to his wife and they shall become one flesh' (Genesis 2:24). So then <b>שְׁאֵרָהּ</b> means closeness of flesh to her. And <b>כְּסוּתָהּ</b> is the covering of her bed, as it is stated (Exodus 22:26): 'for it is his covering alone, in what shall he lie?' And <b>עוֹנָתָהּ</b> is the season (עוֹנָה) that he comes to her at the time of lovemaking.\n\nAnd if the meaning of שְׁאֵר were simply 'flesh' as the commentators say (Rashi and Radak on Psalms 78:27, and Radak here), and we were to say that 'to all שְׁאֵר of his flesh' (Leviticus 18:6) means 'flesh of his flesh,' in the sense of what is stated (Genesis 37:27): 'for he is our brother, our flesh' — we would likewise explain <b>שְׁאֵרָהּ לֹא יִגְרָע</b> as meaning that he shall not withhold from her flesh, that is, the flesh proper to her, which is the husband's flesh that is with her as one flesh. And the sense of Scripture is that if he takes another, the closeness of flesh of this one and the covering of her bed and her time of lovemaking shall not be diminished from her, for so is the judgment of daughters. And the reason is that the other should not sit with him on an honored bed and they be as one flesh there, while this one lies with him as a concubine by chance encounter and on the ground like coming to a harlot woman — therefore Scripture forbade him this. And so the Sages said (Ketuvot 48a): שְׁאֵרָהּ — this is closeness of flesh, that he must not conduct himself with her after the custom of Persians who have relations with their wives in their garments. And this is a correct interpretation, for the way of Scripture in every place is to mention the act of lying together in clean language and with brevity, and therefore in these matters it spoke in allusion: <b>שְׁאֵרָהּ כְּסוּתָהּ וְעוֹנָתָהּ</b> — for the three matters that a man has with his wife in their union. And this will fit properly with the law of the halakhah, and the food-allowance and the woman's clothing will be a Rabbinic enactment."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Ramban opens with a peshat reading of 'כְּמִשְׁפַּט הַבָּנוֹת' as the father- purchaser's obligation if he designates the maid servant for his son, then shifts to the Sages' reading. He then disputes Rashi's rendering of 'שְׁאֵרָהּ' as food-allowance by arguing from peshat and language. The name 'ר\"א' is resolved as Rabbi Eliyahu (i.e., Ibn Ezra) based on his interpretation of this verse. References to the commentators (רש\"י והרד\"ק) are editorial parentheticals in the Hebrew text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:13:50.917197278Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}